nored but still logged as \\\"committed\\\", and the blocked link stays staged so every later backup fails silently; (2) minor \\u2014 text still says `.gitignore` where it should say `.git/info/exclude`; (3) minor \\u2014 the Pi test user may inherit the `staff` group, stalling that boundary step.\\n- User said \\\"pause\\\" (during an API ENOTFOUND error), then \\\"resume\\\".\\n\\n**Decisions Made:**\\n- Third-party vendored symlinks (`claudex-loop`, `claudex-route`, `codex-build`, `codex-review`) stay **out** of the `claude-skills` repo \\u2014 same rule as plugins. Windows needs a separate install of that package. They were already committed and pushed as dead links on Windows; now explicit rather than silent.\\n- Task 4's inversion script deleted entirely \\u2014 leave the 19 third-party links alone rather than copy them in.\\n- Task 2 prune executed as a **move, not delete** (reversible).\\n\\n**Lessons Learned:**\\n- **`.gitignore` patterns ending in `/` match directories but NOT symlinks** \\u2014 the trailing slash would have silently re-added all four dead links on the next backup run.\\n- Backups must be verified by **actually restoring them** \\u2014 the restore returned 57/61 skills, exposing a two-hop symlink chain (`~/.claude/skills/X \\u2192 ~/.agents/skills/X \\u2192 ~/.agents/vendor-review/...`) into an unbacked-up vendor folder.\\n- Re-run the review gate after amending a plan \\u2014 the prior approval is stale. Codex found 5 new bugs in the amendment itself.\\n- `dcg` blocks `mv` with variable paths; Python with explicit containment checks is the workaround.\\n- Directory counts can mismatch because `ls` omits hidden files (209 vs 208 was a hidden `.gitignore`).\\n- A second reviewer with a different provider catches different classes of error \\u2014 the provider switch wasn't just a fallback, it found the plan's biggest error.\\n\\n**Action Items:**\\n- Fix the three round-10 findings: make the backup report a failed commit, have the sync unstage third-party links, correct `.gitignore` \\u2192 `.git/info/exclude` wording, handle the `staff`-group inheritance in the Pi boundary step.\\n- Re-run the review gate, then start Tasks 3\\u20138.\\n- Windows: separately install the vendored package that provides `claudex-loop`, `claudex-route`, `codex-build`, `codex-review` (currently dead symlinks there).\\n- Task 2 result to record: global skills pool pruned 306 \\u2192 208; 61 skills still resolve; ~150 third-party installs untouched.\\n\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"uv run python hooks/session-start.py","durationMs":862},"type":"attachment","uuid":"0be12f4c-975e-4867-b17d-54e67a317377","timestamp":"2026-09-13T04:30:28.612Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"d290860c-34be-4b50-ac6a-051bd16fb6c5","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"0be12f4c-975e-4867-b17d-54e67a317377","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"0f0e2f8d-d651-4ec1-abd6-8e7584892f0f","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"SessionStart\",\"additionalContext\":\"\\n<context_window_protection>\\n  <priority_instructions>\\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\\n  </priority_instructions>\\n\\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \\\"timeline\\\")\\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\\n       - Each command: {label: \\\"section header\\\", command: \\\"shell command\\\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\\\"q1\\\", \\\"q2\\\", ...])\\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n\\n  <when_not_to_use>\\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\\n  </when_not_to_use>\\n\\n  <file_writing_policy>\\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\\n  </file_writing_policy>\\n\\n  <output_constraints>\\n    <artifact_policy>\\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\\n    </artifact_policy>\\n  </output_constraints>\\n  <session_continuity>\\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\\n  </session_continuity>\\n\\n  <ctx_commands>\\n    \\\"ctx stats\\\" | \\\"ctx-stats\\\" | \\\"/ctx-stats\\\" | context savings question\\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx doctor\\\" | \\\"ctx-doctor\\\" | \\\"/ctx-doctor\\\" | diagnose context-mode\\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx upgrade\\\" | \\\"ctx-upgrade\\\" | \\\"/ctx-upgrade\\\" | update context-mode\\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx purge\\\" | \\\"ctx-purge\\\" | \\\"/ctx-purge\\\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\\n\\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \\\"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\\\"\\n  </ctx_commands>\\n\\n</context_window_protection>\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/sessionstart.mjs\"","durationMs":1581},"type":"attachment","uuid":"a96b1b20-c041-41f2-af8b-863476000ca9","timestamp":"2026-09-13T04:30:29.332Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"d290860c-34be-4b50-ac6a-051bd16fb6c5","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"a96b1b20-c041-41f2-af8b-863476000ca9","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"0f0e2f8d-d651-4ec1-abd6-8e7584892f0f","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\n  \"hookSpecificOutput\": {\n    \"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\",\n    \"additionalContext\": \"<EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\\nYou have superpowers.\\n\\n**Below is the full content of your 'superpowers:using-superpowers' skill - your introduction to using skills. For all other skills, use the 'Skill' tool:**\\n\\n---\\nname: using-superpowers\\ndescription: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\\n---\\n\\n<SUBAGENT-STOP>\\nIf you were dispatched as a subagent to execute a specific task, ignore this skill.\\n</SUBAGENT-STOP>\\n\\n<EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\\nIf you think there is even a 1% chance a skill might apply to what you are doing, you ABSOLUTELY MUST invoke the skill.\\n\\nIF A SKILL APPLIES TO YOUR TASK, YOU DO NOT HAVE A CHOICE. YOU MUST USE IT.\\n\\nThis is not negotiable. You cannot rationalize your way out of this.\\n</EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\\n\\n## The Rule\\n\\n**Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action** — including clarifying questions, exploring the codebase, or checking files. If it turns out wrong for the situation, you don't have to use it.\\n\\n**Before entering plan mode:** if you haven't already brainstormed, invoke the brainstorming skill first.\\n\\nThen announce \\\"Using [skill] to [purpose]\\\" and follow the skill exactly. If it has a checklist, create a todo per item.\\n\\n## Skill Priority\\n\\nWhen multiple skills apply, process skills come first — they set the approach, then implementation skills (frontend-design, etc.) carry it out. Brainstorming and systematic-debugging are Superpowers' most common process skills, but the rule holds for any of them.\\n\\n- \\\"Let's build X\\\" → superpowers:brainstorming first, then implementation skills.\\n- \\\"Fix this bug\\\" → superpowers:systematic-debugging first, then domain skills.\\n\\n## Red Flags\\n\\nThese thoughts mean STOP—you're rationalizing:\\n\\n| Thought | Reality |\\n|---------|---------|\\n| \\\"This is just a simple question\\\" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"I need more context first\\\" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |\\n| \\\"Let me explore the codebase first\\\" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |\\n| \\\"I can check git/files quickly\\\" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"Let me gather information first\\\" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |\\n| \\\"This doesn't need a formal skill\\\" | If a skill exists, use it. |\\n| \\\"I remember this skill\\\" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |\\n| \\\"This doesn't count as a task\\\" | Action = task. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"The skill is overkill\\\" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |\\n| \\\"I'll just do this one thing first\\\" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |\\n| \\\"This feels productive\\\" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |\\n| \\\"I know what that means\\\" | Knowing the concept ≠ using the skill. Invoke it. |\\n\\n## Platform Adaptation\\n\\nIf your harness appears here, read its reference file for special instructions:\\n\\n- Codex: `references/codex-tools.md`\\n- Pi: `references/pi-tools.md`\\n- Antigravity: `references/antigravity-tools.md`\\n- Hermes Agent: `references/hermes-tools.md`\\n\\n## User Instructions\\n\\nUser instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md, etc, direct requests) take precedence over skills, which in turn override default behavior. Only skip skill workflows or instructions when your human partner has explicitly told you to.\\n</EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\"\n  }\n}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"\"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/run-hook.cmd\" session-start","durationMs":1775},"type":"attachment","uuid":"faa8edcb-c092-4afe-83d2-b874c01f6f81","timestamp":"2026-09-13T04:30:29.525Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"d290860c-34be-4b50-ac6a-051bd16fb6c5","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"faa8edcb-c092-4afe-83d2-b874c01f6f81","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"0f0e2f8d-d651-4ec1-abd6-8e7584892f0f","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"/last30days: Ready to use. Run /last30days to get started — setup takes 30 seconds.\n  Research any topic across Reddit, HN, X, YouTube, Polymarket (last 30 days).\n\nReddit, Hacker News, Polymarket, and YouTube (yt-dlp detected) work out of the box.\nThe setup wizard can unlock X/Twitter and more.\n  Detected: yt-dlp is installed (YouTube transcripts ready, no setup needed).","stdout":"/last30days: Ready to use. Run /last30days to get started — setup takes 30 seconds.\n  Research any topic across Reddit, HN, X, YouTube, Polymarket (last 30 days).\n\nReddit, Hacker News, Polymarket, and YouTube (yt-dlp detected) work out of the box.\nThe setup wizard can unlock X/Twitter and more.\n  Detected: yt-dlp is installed (YouTube transcripts ready, no setup needed).\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"bash \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/scripts/check-config.sh\"","durationMs":2157},"type":"attachment","uuid":"ceaf61b5-89e7-4f55-9ea0-4ad27e2de8cc","timestamp":"2026-09-13T04:30:29.908Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"d290860c-34be-4b50-ac6a-051bd16fb6c5","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"ceaf61b5-89e7-4f55-9ea0-4ad27e2de8cc","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_additional_context","content":["## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\n# User Profile\n\n## About\nEric Rash — Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds athlete performance data pipelines (Football GPS, force plate). Family: wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo as of 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-forward player-coach near Iowa, ~$90K+ floor, ~mid-2027. Deeper context: `~/DEV/ContextVault` — read before career/personal advice.\n\n## Preferences\n- Python + R. **macOS since 2026-08-05**; Windows 11 box still ACTIVE (synced 9/9). Tool paths: see CLAUDE.md.\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB; no cloud/servers/schedulers without Baylor IT approval. OneDrive project folders — beware DuckDB locks.\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\n\n## Working Style\n- Direct; says \"stop\" when done. Wants to be challenged, not affirmed — push on vague or rehearsed reasoning (\"sharpened, not soothed\").\n- Defers technical micro-decisions when convention exists — decide, state it, move on.\n- Builds incrementally. Nothing that breaks mid-season.\n- Authors own direction when leadership doesn't; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo org.\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\n\n---\n\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\n# Working Memory\n\n## Active Threads\n- **Personal site (9/2)**: `DEV/Website/scrollcraft/builds/eric-rash/`, pushed 9/9. NEXT: real-phone check; host or keep local. →Tier A personal-site-build-routes.\n- **Baseball profiles (9/2)**: `DEV/Baseball_PerformanceProfiles`, Excel-only coach. 3 .xlsx mock-data prototypes. NEXT: coach picks one + real tests.\n- **RTP CLA course (9/10)**: ingest + PDF rebuild DONE. NEXT: import .ris → Zotero; lesson 0002. →Tier A rtp-cla-course.\n- **ATH Tech framework (8/14)**: shipped. NEXT: Micah mtg (need ITS intake path); inventory refresh due 9/29. →Tier A ath-tech-framework, tech-inventory-refresh.\n- **Kinexon volleyball (8/1)**: API email→CSM, awaiting docs. Plan: sync→SQLite→dashboards; CSV interim.\n- **Status report (9/7)**: #2 drafted, UNSENT — `SecondBrain/Inbox/status-reports/2026-09-07-email.txt`. NEXT: read-check, send, rename `-email-sent.txt`, flip `sent: true`. #3 = 9/21. →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline.\n- **Acro & Tumbling (9/8)**: owe an Excel \"pass builder\" — routines → cumulative impact load. 5-athlete pilot. Awaiting skill inventory + assignments.\n- **Open PRs (9/7)**: AgentAssist #1 (draft), CFB_Analytics_Lab #1. Deferred: FA26 camp regex.\n- **OKR/JD 2026 (7/17)**: Blake LEFT, AJ owns FB. →vault `Projects/Applied-Performance-JD-2026/`.\n- Parked in Tier A: Fireflies key rotation; child dental.\n\n- **Skill audit (9/12)**: DONE. 160→61 global skills, 19.2k→6.4k tok/session. Plugins beat local copies. →Tier A skill-bloat-and-promotion\n\n## Environment Notes\n- **Portfolio dashboard** LIVE; `/project` re-renders+republishes. →Tier A command-centre, portfolio-registry-write-provenance.\n- **Terminal (9/4)**: tmux = default VS Code shell; `cc`/`cx` aliases. →vault `Knowledge/tooling/tmux-cheat-sheet.md`.\n- Hooks exist in TWO copies (`.claude/hooks/`, `.codex/hooks/`) — patch both. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo (`erash11/DEV`), 8 router `.md` only; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. `_ARCHIVE/` = parked. →Tier A github-consolidation.\n- Schedules/gws limits →automation-cadence. CFB capture (Mon, 8/24–1/23) →cfb-forecast-capture.\n\n## Pending Decisions\n- **DUE (was 8/31)**: OKR Q1 gate: AI integration (buy-in) + data governance (Anthony's proposal).\n- **Kitman vs MyLink**: MyLink intros (~half Kitman's cost) + buy Kitman API access. Undecided; blocks priority 3.\n\n---\n\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\n\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (5670 chars, updated 2026-09-12 13:35)","## Today\nSaturday, September 12, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n260 compiled articles (195 concepts, 64 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n### Session (17:31)\n\n**Context:** Continuing the multi-task \"harness-agnostic AIOS\" plan (`~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md`, pushed as `00730b6`) — making the skills/backup stack work on both Mac and Windows. Tasks 1–2 complete; paused, then resumed at round 10 of plan review.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Codex hit its usage limit mid-gate (reset 4:46 PM). User said \"use claude, that fine\" → reviewer switched to Claude via `--host codex`, which starts a fresh review session (resume can't cross providers).\n- The Claude reviewer found what Codex missed: **14 of 19** outward symlinks in the skills repo are third-party package-manager installs (including 5 `mattpocock-skills` explicitly out of scope). Task 4 would have copied all of them into Eric's personal repo.\n- Reframed the inventory: **42 skills Eric wrote, 19 third-party links.**\n- Round-10 review findings (plan not yet approved): (1) serious — the new safety hook silently breaks the daily backup: a refused commit is ignored but still logged as \"committed\", and the blocked link stays staged so every later backup fails silently; (2) minor — text still says `.gitignore` where it should say `.git/info/exclude`; (3) minor — the Pi test user may inherit the `staff` group, stalling that boundary step.\n- User said \"pause\" (during an API ENOTFOUND error), then \"resume\".\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Third-party vendored symlinks (`claudex-loop`, `claudex-route`, `codex-build`, `codex-review`) stay **out** of the `claude-skills` repo — same rule as plugins. Windows needs a separate install of that package. They were already committed and pushed as dead links on Windows; now explicit rather than silent.\n- Task 4's inversion script deleted entirely — leave the 19 third-party links alone rather than copy them in.\n- Task 2 prune executed as a **move, not delete** (reversible).\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- **`.gitignore` patterns ending in `/` match directories but NOT symlinks** — the trailing slash would have silently re-added all four dead links on the next backup run.\n- Backups must be verified by **actually restoring them** — the restore returned 57/61 skills, exposing a two-hop symlink chain (`~/.claude/skills/X → ~/.agents/skills/X → ~/.agents/vendor-review/...`) into an unbacked-up vendor folder.\n- Re-run the review gate after amending a plan — the prior approval is stale. Codex found 5 new bugs in the amendment itself.\n- `dcg` blocks `mv` with variable paths; Python with explicit containment checks is the workaround.\n- Directory counts can mismatch because `ls` omits hidden files (209 vs 208 was a hidden `.gitignore`).\n- A second reviewer with a different provider catches different classes of error — the provider switch wasn't just a fallback, it found the plan's biggest error.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Fix the three round-10 findings: make the backup report a failed commit, have the sync unstage third-party links, correct `.gitignore` → `.git/info/exclude` wording, handle the `staff`-group inheritance in the Pi boundary step.\n- Re-run the review gate, then start Tasks 3–8.\n- Windows: separately install the vendored package that provides `claudex-loop`, `claudex-route`, `codex-build`, `codex-review` (currently dead symlinks there).\n- Task 2 result to record: global skills pool pruned 306 → 208; 61 skills still resolve; ~150 third-party installs untouched.\n","## Today\nSaturday, September 12, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n260 compiled articles (195 concepts, 64 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n### Session (17:31)\n\n**Context:** Continuing the multi-task \"harness-agnostic AIOS\" plan (`~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md`, pushed as `00730b6`) — making the skills/backup stack work on both Mac and Windows. Tasks 1–2 complete; paused, then resumed at round 10 of plan review.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Codex hit its usage limit mid-gate (reset 4:46 PM). User said \"use claude, that fine\" → reviewer switched to Claude via `--host codex`, which starts a fresh review session (resume can't cross providers).\n- The Claude reviewer found what Codex missed: **14 of 19** outward symlinks in the skills repo are third-party package-manager installs (including 5 `mattpocock-skills` explicitly out of scope). Task 4 would have copied all of them into Eric's personal repo.\n- Reframed the inventory: **42 skills Eric wrote, 19 third-party links.**\n- Round-10 review findings (plan not yet approved): (1) serious — the new safety hook silently breaks the daily backup: a refused commit is ignored but still logged as \"committed\", and the blocked link stays staged so every later backup fails silently; (2) minor — text still says `.gitignore` where it should say `.git/info/exclude`; (3) minor — the Pi test user may inherit the `staff` group, stalling that boundary step.\n- User said \"pause\" (during an API ENOTFOUND error), then \"resume\".\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Third-party vendored symlinks (`claudex-loop`, `claudex-route`, `codex-build`, `codex-review`) stay **out** of the `claude-skills` repo — same rule as plugins. Windows needs a separate install of that package. They were already committed and pushed as dead links on Windows; now explicit rather than silent.\n- Task 4's inversion script deleted entirely — leave the 19 third-party links alone rather than copy them in.\n- Task 2 prune executed as a **move, not delete** (reversible).\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- **`.gitignore` patterns ending in `/` match directories but NOT symlinks** — the trailing slash would have silently re-added all four dead links on the next backup run.\n- Backups must be verified by **actually restoring them** — the restore returned 57/61 skills, exposing a two-hop symlink chain (`~/.claude/skills/X → ~/.agents/skills/X → ~/.agents/vendor-review/...`) into an unbacked-up vendor folder.\n- Re-run the review gate after amending a plan — the prior approval is stale. Codex found 5 new bugs in the amendment itself.\n- `dcg` blocks `mv` with variable paths; Python with explicit containment checks is the workaround.\n- Directory counts can mismatch because `ls` omits hidden files (209 vs 208 was a hidden `.gitignore`).\n- A second reviewer with a different provider catches different classes of error — the provider switch wasn't just a fallback, it found the plan's biggest error.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Fix the three round-10 findings: make the backup report a failed commit, have the sync unstage third-party links, correct `.gitignore` → `.git/info/exclude` wording, handle the `staff`-group inheritance in the Pi boundary step.\n- Re-run the review gate, then start Tasks 3–8.\n- Windows: separately install the vendored package that provides `claudex-loop`, `claudex-route`, `codex-build`, `codex-review` (currently dead symlinks there).\n- Task 2 result to record: global skills pool pruned 306 → 208; 61 skills still resolve; ~150 third-party installs untouched.\n","\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n  <ctx_commands>\n    \"ctx stats\" | \"ctx-stats\" | \"/ctx-stats\" | context savings question\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\n\n    \"ctx doctor\" | \"ctx-doctor\" | \"/ctx-doctor\" | diagnose context-mode\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx upgrade\" | \"ctx-upgrade\" | \"/ctx-upgrade\" | update context-mode\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx purge\" | \"ctx-purge\" | \"/ctx-purge\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\n\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\"\n  </ctx_commands>\n\n</context_window_protection>","<EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\nYou have superpowers.\n\n**Below is the full content of your 'superpowers:using-superpowers' skill - your introduction to using skills. For all other skills, use the 'Skill' tool:**\n\n---\nname: using-superpowers\ndescription: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\n---\n\n<SUBAGENT-STOP>\nIf you were dispatched as a subagent to execute a specific task, ignore this skill.\n</SUBAGENT-STOP>\n\n<EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\nIf you think there is even a 1% chance a skill might apply to what you are doing, you ABSOLUTELY MUST invoke the skill.\n\nIF A SKILL APPLIES TO YOUR TASK, YOU DO NOT HAVE A CHOICE. YOU MUST USE IT.\n\nThis is not negotiable. You cannot rationalize your way out of this.\n</EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\n\n## The Rule\n\n**Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action** — including clarifying questions, exploring the codebase, or checking files. If it turns out wrong for the situation, you don't have to use it.\n\n**Before entering plan mode:** if you haven't already brainstormed, invoke the brainstorming skill first.\n\nThen announce \"Using [skill] to [purpose]\" and follow the skill exactly. If it has a checklist, create a todo per item.\n\n## Skill Priority\n\nWhen multiple skills apply, process skills come first — they set the approach, then implementation skills (frontend-design, etc.) carry it out. Brainstorming and systematic-debugging are Superpowers' most common process skills, but the rule holds for any of them.\n\n- \"Let's build X\" → superpowers:brainstorming first, then implementation skills.\n- \"Fix this bug\" → superpowers:systematic-debugging first, then domain skills.\n\n## Red Flags\n\nThese thoughts mean STOP—you're rationalizing:\n\n| Thought | Reality |\n|---------|---------|\n| \"This is just a simple question\" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |\n| \"I need more context first\" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |\n| \"Let me explore the codebase first\" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |\n| \"I can check git/files quickly\" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |\n| \"Let me gather information first\" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |\n| \"This doesn't need a formal skill\" | If a skill exists, use it. |\n| \"I remember this skill\" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |\n| \"This doesn't count as a task\" | Action = task. Check for skills. |\n| \"The skill is overkill\" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |\n| \"I'll just do this one thing first\" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |\n| \"This feels productive\" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |\n| \"I know what that means\" | Knowing the concept ≠ using the skill. Invoke it. |\n\n## Platform Adaptation\n\nIf your harness appears here, read its reference file for special instructions:\n\n- Codex: `references/codex-tools.md`\n- Pi: `references/pi-tools.md`\n- Antigravity: `references/antigravity-tools.md`\n- Hermes Agent: `references/hermes-tools.md`\n\n## User Instructions\n\nUser instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md, etc, direct requests) take precedence over skills, which in turn override default behavior. Only skip skill workflows or instructions when your human partner has explicitly told you to.\n</EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>"],"hookName":"SessionStart","toolUseID":"SessionStart","hookEvent":"SessionStart"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"beddab3a-a6b6-4981-8e04-93b95545488d","timestamp":"2026-09-13T04:30:29.911Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"d290860c-34be-4b50-ac6a-051bd16fb6c5","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"beddab3a-a6b6-4981-8e04-93b95545488d","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"e1d3ed36-2248-45c6-be3f-039b2c23acd1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"Review the conversation context below and respond with a concise summary\nof important items that should be preserved in the daily log.\nDo NOT use any tools — just return plain text.\n\nFormat your response as a structured daily log entry with these sections:\n\n**Context:** [One line about what the user was working on]\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- [Important Q&A or discussions]\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- [Any decisions with rationale]\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- [Gotchas, patterns, or insights discovered]\n\n**Action Items:**\n- [Follow-ups or TODOs mentioned]\n\nSkip anything that is:\n- Routine tool calls or file reads\n- Content that's trivial or obvious\n- Trivial back-and-forth or clarification exchanges\n\nOnly include sections that have actual content. If nothing is worth saving,\nrespond with exactly: FLUSH_OK\n\n## Conversation Context\n\n**User:** <command-message>backup</command-message>\n<command-name>/backup</command-name>\n\n**User:** Base directory for this skill: /Users/kristinarash/.claude/skills/backup\n\n# Backup\n\nRuns `~/.claude/scripts/daily-backup.ps1` right now. Same script the `com.keystone.dailybackup`\nlaunchd agent runs at 09:00 daily — this is the on-demand path for when the Mac was shut at 09:00.\n\n## Run it\n\nDo not pre-check anything. Run first, report after.\n\n```bash\n/opt/homebrew/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -File ~/.claude/scripts/daily-backup.ps1 2>&1 | tail -40\n```\n\nUse a Bash `timeout` of at least `600000` (10 min). The memsearch INDEX step is ~75s of a ~85s\nhealthy run; the default 2-min timeout can survive it, but a grown corpus will not.\n\n## Read the verdict from state, not from the tail\n\n```bash\ncat ~/.claude/scripts/logs/backup-state.json\n```\n\n`status` is `OK` or `ERROR`; `errors` lists the failed job codes. That file is the authority.\nThe tail is for diagnosing, not for deciding.\n\n**`status: OK` is not \"everything was saved\" — read `okCount` too.** A run that skipped the\nSSD still ends `OK` with 0 errors. See the SSD gotcha below for what the count means.\n\n## Then clear the stale hook error, if any\n\nA failed run appends a line to `~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log`, which is surfaced\nas a HOOK FAILURE at every session start. A later successful run does **not** clear it. If `status`\nis now `OK` and the file contains only `daily-backup:` lines, archive and empty it:\n\n```bash\ncp ~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log ~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log.archived-$(date +%Y-%m-%d)\ncp /dev/null ~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log\n```\n\nIf any non-`daily-backup` line is present, leave the file alone and tell the user what else is in it.\nShell truncation (`: > file`) under `$HOME` is blocked by dcg rule\n`core.filesystem:redirect-truncate-root-home`, and there is no `truncate` binary here — use `cp /dev/null`.\n\n## Report\n\nThree lines, plain: did it work, what was skipped, what to do next. Never report \"backup complete\"\nwithout naming a skipped target — a silent partial backup is the failure this skill exists to prevent.\n\n## Gotchas\n\n**Safe to ignore — expected on a healthy run:**\n- `SNAP skip (missing): ~/.codex/memories` — that path has never existed.\n- `TARGET baylor deprecated in manifest - snapshots to it SKIPPED` — deliberate.\n\n**Real gaps — always surface these to the user:**\n- `TARGET SSD /Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup NOT MOUNTED - orphan and working-tree capture SKIPPED`\n  — the run still reports `OK` with 0 errors, but **untracked, gitignored, and uncommitted-modified\n  files were not saved anywhere.** GitHub and Google Drive cover only committed and pushed work.\n  Tell the user to plug in CRASHBOX and run again. This is the most likely way to lose real work.\n\n  **Cross-check with `okCount`, because the error count cannot see this.** `okCount` in\n  `backup-state.json` is the number of jobs that ran, and two whole job classes — `CAPTURE`\n  (per-project working-tree files) and `STATE-SSD` — exist only when the SSD is mounted. So:\n  - **~31 jobs** = SSD captured. **~9-10 jobs** = SSD was absent; only the 4 `REPO` pushes and\n    6 Drive `SNAP`s ran (verified against runs on 2026-09-04 and 2026-09-05).\n  - A single-digit `okCount` with `status: OK` is the silent partial backup. Say so out loud.\n  - Do not derive this from the error count or the exit code — both read `OK` either way.\n- `TARGET ... NOT MOUNTED - is Google Drive for Desktop running?` — the Drive app is not running.\n  Start it, then re-run.\n- `PUSH ERR ... Could not resolve host: github.com` — a network blip, not a repo problem. Confirm\n  with `ping -c1 github.com`, then just re-run; the commits are already made locally and will push.\n  (Seen 2026-09-04 09:05: both `global-memory` and `skills` failed this way and a re-run fixed both.)\n- `repo-diverged:<name>` — automation will never merge. Reconcile that repo by hand.\n\n**Other:**\n- Every log line appears **twice** for launchd runs and once for manual runs. The script both writes\n  to the log file and prints to stdout, and the plist captures stdout to the same file. Not a bug —\n  do not \"fix\" it, and do not read doubled lines as two attempts.\n- The script is PowerShell only because `pwsh` runs it natively. It manages launchd, not Task\n  Scheduler; the Windows original is parked at `daily-backup.ps1.win-orig`.\n- `-DryRun` is the only parameter. Use it to preview without writing.\n\n## Examples\n\n**Example 1: routine catch-up**\n```\nUser: \"run the backup\"\n→ Runs the script, reads backup-state.json (status OK, 0 errors)\n→ \"Backup done, no errors. Everything pushed and copied to Drive.\n   CRASHBOX SSD wasn't plugged in, so uncommitted files were not saved.\n   Plug it in and run /backup again if you have unsaved work.\"\n```\n\n**Example 2: session start shows a hook failure**\n```\nUser: \"the backup failed this morning — fix it\"\n→ Greps daily-backup.log for the error codes → DNS failure on both pushes\n→ Confirms github.com reachable, re-runs the script → status OK\n→ Archives and empties .errors.log so the warning stops appearing\n```\n\n**Assistant:** Running the backup script now.\n\n**Assistant:** The backup worked, but only on the second run.\n\n**What went wrong:** The first run failed on one folder, `LibraryProject`. On 9/11, `ZoteroInjestion` became a submodule (a git repo inside another repo). Its `.git` changed from a folder to a small file. The SSD still had the old `.git` folder, so rsync could not copy the new file there. This failed every run while CRASHBOX was plugged in.\n\n**What I did:**\n- I renamed the old SSD folder. I did not delete it. It is now `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup/DEV-nonrepo/LibraryProject/ZoteroInjestion/.git.stale-dir-2026-09-12` (139 MB).\n- I ran the backup again. Result: `status: OK`, 0 errors, `okCount: 80`. 80 is the full count, so the SSD copy ran too.\n- I saved a copy of the old error log, then emptied `.errors.log`. The warning at session start will stop.\n\n**Skipped:** Nothing important. The `baylor` target is turned off on purpose.\n\n**What you do now:** Nothing. You can delete the renamed folder at any time. It is an old copy, and GitHub has the repo.\n\nOne small thing: the backup list still says `LibraryProject` is not a repo. It is one now, with a GitHub remote. Backups still work. Tell me if you want me to fix that entry.\n\nresult: Backup is clean (OK, 0 errors, 80 jobs, SSD included); I fixed the LibraryProject SSD failure and cleared the hook error."},"uuid":"d07b9130-81d4-4c90-aec1-e0509d66b435","timestamp":"2026-09-13T04:30:31.084Z","permissionMode":"auto","promptSource":"sdk","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"d290860c-34be-4b50-ac6a-051bd16fb6c5","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"d07b9130-81d4-4c90-aec1-e0509d66b435","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"deferred_tools_delta","addedNames":["CronCreate","CronDelete","CronList","DesignSync","EnterWorktree","ExitWorktree","ListMcpResourcesTool","Monitor","NotebookEdit","PushNotification","ReadMcpResourceDirTool","ReadMcpResourceTool","RemoteTrigger","SendMessage","TaskCreate","TaskGet","TaskList","TaskOutput","TaskStop","TaskUpdate","WebFetch","WebSearch","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_doctor","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_index","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_insight","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_purge","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_stats","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_upgrade","mcp__zotero__analytics","mcp__zotero__cache","mcp__zotero__cache_index_fulltext","mcp__zotero__cache_reindex","mcp__zotero__cache_status","mcp__zotero__cite","mcp__zotero__collections_get","mcp__zotero__collections_items","mcp__zotero__collections_list","mcp__zotero__collections_subcollections","mcp__zotero__collections_top","mcp__zotero__collections_tree","mcp__zotero__context","mcp__zotero__deleted_list","mcp__zotero__export","mcp__zotero__full_text_deep_search","mcp__zotero__fulltext_changed","mcp__zotero__ground","mcp__zotero__items_children","mcp__zotero__items_fulltext","mcp__zotero__items_get","mcp__zotero__items_list","mcp__zotero__items_recent","mcp__zotero__items_top","mcp__zotero__items_trash","mcp__zotero__keys_current","mcp__zotero__learnings","mcp__zotero__learnings_candidates","mcp__zotero__learnings_confirm","mcp__zotero__learnings_forget","mcp__zotero__learnings_list","mcp__zotero__learnings_reject","mcp__zotero__learnings_stats","mcp__zotero__playbook","mcp__zotero__playbook_amend","mcp__zotero__playbook_list","mcp__zotero__recall","mcp__zotero__search","mcp__zotero__searches_get","mcp__zotero__searches_list","mcp__zotero__sql","mcp__zotero__sync","mcp__zotero__tags_list","mcp__zotero__tail","mcp__zotero__teach","mcp__zotero__teach_lookup","mcp__zotero__teach_pattern","mcp__zotero__teach_playbook","mcp__zotero__workflow","mcp__zotero__workflow_archive","mcp__zotero__workflow_status"],"addedLines":["CronCreate","CronDelete","CronList","DesignSync","EnterWorktree","ExitWorktree","ListMcpResourcesTool","Monitor","NotebookEdit","PushNotification","ReadMcpResourceDirTool","ReadMcpResourceTool","RemoteTrigger","SendMessage","TaskCreate","TaskGet","TaskList","TaskOutput","TaskStop","TaskUpdate","WebFetch","WebSearch","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_doctor","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_index","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_insight","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_purge","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_stats","mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_upgrade","mcp__zotero__analytics","mcp__zotero__cache","mcp__zotero__cache_index_fulltext","mcp__zotero__cache_reindex","mcp__zotero__cache_status","mcp__zotero__cite","mcp__zotero__collections_get","mcp__zotero__collections_items","mcp__zotero__collections_list","mcp__zotero__collections_subcollections","mcp__zotero__collections_top","mcp__zotero__collections_tree","mcp__zotero__context","mcp__zotero__deleted_list","mcp__zotero__export","mcp__zotero__full_text_deep_search","mcp__zotero__fulltext_changed","mcp__zotero__ground","mcp__zotero__items_children","mcp__zotero__items_fulltext","mcp__zotero__items_get","mcp__zotero__items_list","mcp__zotero__items_recent","mcp__zotero__items_top","mcp__zotero__items_trash","mcp__zotero__keys_current","mcp__zotero__learnings","mcp__zotero__learnings_candidates","mcp__zotero__learnings_confirm","mcp__zotero__learnings_forget","mcp__zotero__learnings_list","mcp__zotero__learnings_reject","mcp__zotero__learnings_stats","mcp__zotero__playbook","mcp__zotero__playbook_amend","mcp__zotero__playbook_list","mcp__zotero__recall","mcp__zotero__search","mcp__zotero__searches_get","mcp__zotero__searches_list","mcp__zotero__sql","mcp__zotero__sync","mcp__zotero__tags_list","mcp__zotero__tail","mcp__zotero__teach","mcp__zotero__teach_lookup","mcp__zotero__teach_pattern","mcp__zotero__teach_playbook","mcp__zotero__workflow","mcp__zotero__workflow_archive","mcp__zotero__workflow_status"],"removedNames":[],"readdedNames":[],"pendingMcpServers":[],"needsAuthMcpServers":[]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"f3d917e2-a6c4-4e5d-952d-c68cfd3a615c","timestamp":"2026-09-13T04:30:31.079Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"d290860c-34be-4b50-ac6a-051bd16fb6c5","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"f3d917e2-a6c4-4e5d-952d-c68cfd3a615c","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"agent_listing_delta","addedTypes":["claude","codex:codex-rescue","Explore","general-purpose","Plan","statusline-setup"],"addedLines":["- claude: Catch-all for any task that doesn't fit a more specific agent. FleetView's default when no agent name is typed. (Tools: *)","- codex:codex-rescue: Proactively use when Claude Code is stuck, wants a second implementation or diagnosis pass, needs a deeper root-cause investigation, or should hand a substantial coding task to Codex through the shared runtime (Tools: Bash)","- Explore: Read-only search agent for broad fan-out searches — when answering means sweeping many files, directories, or naming conventions and you only need the conclusion, not the file dumps. It reads excerpts rather than whole files, so it locates code; it doesn't review or audit it. Specify search breadth: \"medium\" for moderate exploration, \"very thorough\" for multiple locations and naming conventions. (Tools: All tools except Agent, Artifact, ExitPlanMode, Edit, Write, NotebookEdit)","- general-purpose: General-purpose agent for researching complex questions, searching for code, and executing multi-step tasks. When you are searching for a keyword or file and are not confident that you will find the right match in the first few tries use this agent to perform the search for you. (Tools: *)","- Plan: Software architect agent for designing implementation plans. Use this when you need to plan the implementation strategy for a task. Returns step-by-step plans, identifies critical files, and considers architectural trade-offs. (Tools: All tools except Agent, Artifact, ExitPlanMode, Edit, Write, NotebookEdit)","- statusline-setup: Use this agent to configure the user's Claude Code status line setting. (Tools: Read, Edit)"],"removedTypes":[],"isInitial":true,"showConcurrencyNote":true},"type":"attachment","uuid":"e18e439d-2bcf-4424-9486-dfef457c2485","timestamp":"2026-09-13T04:30:31.079Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"d290860c-34be-4b50-ac6a-051bd16fb6c5","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"e18e439d-2bcf-4424-9486-dfef457c2485","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"skill_listing","content":"- anti-sleep: Keep the user's MacBook awake reliably with macOS caffeinate for a set duration or while a process runs. Use for \"don't let my Mac sleep\", \"keep the screen on\", \"anti-sleep\", \"caffeinate\", overnight work, or long builds. Unlike a normal background job, it survives temporary agent-shell cleanup.\n- backup: Run the KeystoneOS daily backup on demand (git-push the memory repos, rsync state to Google Drive and the CRASHBOX SSD, reindex memsearch), then report what was saved and what was skipped. Use when the user says \"/backup\", \"run the backup\", \"back up now\", \"back my stuff up\", \"did the backup run\", \"the backup failed\", \"I left the laptop off — catch up the backup\", or when a session-start HOOK FAILURE names daily-backup. The scheduled launchd job only fires if the Mac is awake at 09:00, so this is the manual catch-up path. NOT FOR whole-stack health checks (use claude-stack-doctor).\n- brain-audit: Score the second brain as a system — the four C's (context, connections, capabilities, cadence) — write a dated report to ~/SecondBrain/audits/, append the score to a trend table, and end by proposing exactly two next builds. Use when the user says \"/brain-audit\", \"audit my second brain\", \"score my brain\", \"is my second brain working\", \"how healthy is my system\", \"level up my brain\", \"what should I build next for my brain\", or when a monthly ritual is due. NOT FOR automation plumbing — launchd jobs, venvs, backup freshness (use claude-stack-doctor). NOT FOR note-level rot — stale seeds, duplicates, conflicting claims (use improve-system).\n- claude-stack-doctor: Health-check Eric's automation/memory stack in one pass and verify the Mac-to-Windows handoff works: launchd agents, Python venvs, memsearch, the memory-compiler flush pipeline, backup freshness, and context budget. Use when the user says \"/claude-stack-doctor\", \"stack doctor\", \"is my stack healthy\", \"did my scheduled jobs run\", \"check the memory pipeline\", \"why didn't the backup run\", or when closeout/morning sessions should verify automation health. Do not use to run the backup itself (use backup) or to audit context-engineering practice (use doctor-plus).\n- claudex-loop: Harden a plan with independent Claude/Codex review, then optionally build and cross-inspect it. Start in either Claude Code or Codex: the host plans and the other provider reviews. Use for claudex this plan, claudex-loop, or the legacy crucible trigger; not for trivial edits.\n- claudex-route: Recommend a model and a scoped handoff for a task in Claude Code or Codex. Use when choosing who should handle a task, seeking a second opinion, getting unstuck, or delegating focused work; execute one handoff when requested.\n- cli-hub-meta-skill: Discover agent-native CLIs for professional software. Access the live catalog to find tools for creative workflows, productivity, AI, and more.\n- closeout: End-of-session sign-off ritual. Sweeps loose ends, commits and pushes what needs it, verifies memory capture, writes a cross-harness handoff, and reports \"clear to sign off\" or what's still open. Use when the user says \"/closeout\", \"any loose ends?\", \"am I good to sign off?\", \"close out for the night\", \"closing out\", \"goodnight\", \"wrap up for the day\", or asks for a system health check before leaving. Do not use at the start of a session (use startup) or for a standalone automation health check (use claude-stack-doctor).\n- code-review: Review the changes since a fixed point (commit, branch, tag, or merge-base) along two axes — Standards (does the code follow this repo's documented coding standards?) and Spec (does the code match what the originating issue/PRD asked for?). Runs both reviews in parallel sub-agents and reports them side by side. Use when the user wants to review a branch, a PR, work-in-progress changes, or asks to \"review since X\".\n- codex-build: Have Codex implement a concrete work order and have Claude independently inspect the final changes. Preserves the explicit Codex builder choice; use claudex-loop for host-based planning and automatic reviewer selection.\n- codex-review: Have Codex independently review an existing implementation plan while Claude coordinates a bounded revision loop. For automatic reviewer selection from either host, use claudex-loop with mode=review.\n- CreateSkill: Invoke BEFORE any skill work — creating, modifying, adding a workflow/tool to, renaming, validating, or canonicalizing any skill. Required by skills/CLAUDE.md; the single home for skill authoring in this workspace. Owns the skill lifecycle: scaffold (public TitleCase vs private _ALLCAPS, flat 2-level, Gotchas + Examples, BPE check), validate, canonicalize, test, improve, optimize descriptions. USE WHEN create skill, new skill, make a skill, build a skill, set up a skill, private skill, make a X skill, add a workflow, add a tool, edit/change/update/rename a skill, skill frontmatter, validate skill, check skill, canonicalize, scaffold skill, test skill, improve skill, optimize description, skill not triggering, overtriggering. NOT FOR TypeScript CLI generation (use CreateCLI).\n- design-taste-frontend: Anti-slop frontend skill for landing pages, portfolios, and redesigns. The agent reads the brief, infers the right design direction, and ships interfaces that do not look templated. Real design systems when applicable, audit-first on redesigns, strict pre-flight check.\n- dev-sync: Bring this machine into line with GitHub and load back the context that came with it: locate DEV automatically, pull config and skills, inventory every project, commit and push local work, pull the other machine's work, clone what is missing, report conflicts, then read the repo's STATUS and latest handoff and report where the last session left off. Use when Eric switches machines, sits down to start work, says 'sync DEV', asks whether everything is pushed, or asks where he left off. Do not use when Eric says '/startup' or 'good morning' (use startup, which runs this sync) or to scaffold a new repo (use new-project).\n- doctor-plus: Runs Claude Code's built-in /doctor health check, then audits the workspace against the 6 then-and-now context-engineering shifts Anthropic shipped with the Claude 5 models (rules to judgement, examples to interfaces, upfront to progressive disclosure, repeats to tool descriptions, CLAUDE.md memory to auto-memory, simple specs to rich references). Reports findings first, fixes only on approval. Use when the user says \"/doctor-plus\", \"doctor plus\", \"doctor-plus\", \"extended doctor\", \"then and now audit\", \"context checkup\". Do not use to check launchd jobs, the memory pipeline, or backups (use claude-stack-doctor).\n- fireflies-intake: One-command Fireflies pipeline — sync new meeting transcripts into the SecondBrain vault, rename generically-titled meetings, then report exactly what was ingested. Use when the user says \"/fireflies-intake\", \"ingest my recent fireflies meetings\", \"sync meetings that aren't in the second brain yet\", \"pull in the new meetings\", \"meeting ingestion\", or asks whether all recent meetings have been ingested. Do not use to search what was said in transcripts (use pp-fireflies).\n- five-slides-generator: Generate a '5 Slides in 5 Minutes' PowerPoint deck for Baylor Athletics Applied Performance — research, narrative, pptxgenjs, .pptx build, QA. Use when the user mentions '5 slides,' '5 slides in 5 minutes,' or gives a topic they want a Performance School deck on. Also trigger for a cross-functional staff presentation, a quick sport science deck, or any short educational deck in the established series format (CMJ, GPS, VBT are prior examples). Do not use for Power BI report pages (use pbir-report-builder) or to turn an existing HTML deck into a PDF (use html-to-pdf).\n- forecast-capture: Capture and freeze the CFB Victory Model's pre-game forecasts before kickoff, then report what moved since last week. Run every Wednesday during the season. Use when the user says \"/forecast-capture\", \"capture the forecast\", \"freeze this week's predictions\", \"run the CFB capture\", \"did we capture this week\", or asks whether the weekly model snapshot has been taken. Do not use for live scores, standings, or schedules (use pp-espn).\n- game-film-timestamps: Fix wrong startTime/endTime values in football game film JSON exported from Hudl, DVSport, or similar. Fetches correct wall-clock timestamps from the ESPN API and writes corrected copies. Use when the user mentions fixing or correcting play-by-play timestamps in game film JSON, or has files with \"OFF\", \"DEF\", or \"KICKS\" in the filename and mentions timestamps or time issues. Do not use for live scores or schedules (use pp-espn) or for GPS session data (use pp-catapult-openfield-connect).\n- graphify: Use for any question about a codebase, its architecture, file relationships, or project content — especially when graphify-out/ exists, where the question should be treated as a graphify query first. Turns any input (code, docs, papers, images, videos) into a persistent knowledge graph with god nodes, community detection, and query/path/explain tools. Do not use for a quick lookup of one known file or symbol, or for sport-science literature questions (use sports-science-research-grounding).\n- harvest: Distill a finished project into evergreen Knowledge/ articles in the SecondBrain vault and log the shipped artifact in the output ledger. Use when the user says \"/harvest <project>\", \"harvest this project\", \"distill what we learned from X\", declares a project finished/shipped/done, or when a session ends with a clearly shipped deliverable worth recording. Do not use for a single story or lesson (use capture-insight) or for the end-of-session sign-off (use closeout).\n- html-to-pdf: Render any local HTML report, deck, or dashboard to a print-ready PDF via headless Chrome — replaces the manual Ctrl+P → Save as PDF ritual. Use when the user says \"export this to PDF\", \"print version\", \"PDF for the coaches\", \"make this printable\", or a coach-facing HTML deliverable needs a shareable/printable copy. Do not use to build the report or deck itself (use status-report or five-slides-generator).\n- impeccable: Use when the user wants to design, redesign, shape, critique, audit, polish, clarify, distill, harden, optimize, adapt, animate, colorize, extract, or otherwise improve a frontend interface. Covers websites, landing pages, dashboards, product UI, app shells, components, forms, settings, onboarding, and empty states. Handles UX review, visual hierarchy, information architecture, cognitive load, accessibility, performance, responsive behavior, theming, anti-patterns, typography, fonts, spacing, layout, alignment, color, motion, micro-interactions, UX copy, error states, edge cases, i18n, and reusable design systems or tokens. Also use for bland designs that need to become bolder or more delightful, loud designs that should become quieter, live browser iteration on UI elements, or ambitious visual effects that should feel technically extraordinary. Not for backend-only or non-UI tasks.\n- improve-system: Audit the SecondBrain vault for rot — stale seed notes, conflicting claims, and duplicate notes — and propose merge/archive/update actions. Use when the user says \"/improve-system\", \"audit my notes\", \"audit the vault\", \"find stale/duplicate notes\", or \"clean up my second brain\". Do not use for mining sessions for missed learnings (use session-miner), capturing a win or lesson (use capture-insight), improving a skill (use write-a-skill), or scoring the second brain as a system (use brain-audit).\n- ingest-book: Ingest a book (PDF/EPUB full-text, Kindle highlights, or framework-only from a physical book) into the SecondBrain library as atomized, citable advisor entries. Use when the user wants to \"ingest a book\", \"add a book to my library/advisors\", \"/ingest-book\", or points at a book file for the advisor system. Do not use for articles, transcripts, or general resources (use ingest-resource).\n- ingest-resource: Ingest any article, YouTube link, transcript, PDF, or note into the SecondBrain vault — routes it to Knowledge/ (stable info) or Projects/ (active work), adds a summary block, and cross-links related notes with [[wikilinks]]. Use when the user shares a URL, file, or pasted content and says \"ingest\", \"save this\", \"add to my vault/second brain\", \"/ingest-resource\", or drops a resource with intent to keep it. Do not use for a whole book (use ingest-book) or for a plain fact to remember (use memory-write).\n- interview-me: End-of-workday structured interview in two parts — a 90-second supervisor log of operational facts (equipment, people, blockers) that feeds the biweekly status report, then four reflection questions that distill the day into atomic permanent notes for the SecondBrain Obsidian vault. NOT a journaling app — produces a fact log and curated synthesis notes, not stream-of-consciousness. Use when the user types `/interview-me`, says \"interview me\", \"let's debrief\", \"end of day interview\", \"process my day\", \"wrap up today\", \"log my day\", or otherwise asks to capture their workday for their second brain or for their supervisor update. NOT FOR building the biweekly report itself (use status-report).\n- library-advisor: Consult a grounded domain advisor built from the curated book library; answers only from ingested entries and cites every claim. Use when the user says \"ask my <domain> advisor\", \"/library-advisor <domain> <question>\", \"what would my library say about…\", \"consult my library advisor\", or consults a standing advisor. Domains live in SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/advisors/. NOT FOR the built-in /advisor reviewer command. Do not use to review a domain for gaps or missing coverage, as in \"review my library\" (use library-review).\n- meeting-prep: Pre-meeting readiness brief for a project — what changed, what's open, every deliverable path. Use when the user says \"/meeting-prep <project>\", \"I have a meeting with <person> — are we in a good spot on <project>?\", \"verify we're up to speed before the meeting\", \"get me ready for this afternoon's meeting\", or names an upcoming coach/staff meeting. Do not use for the biweekly supervisor report (use status-report) or to sync meeting transcripts (use meeting-sync).\n- meeting-sync: Sync new Fireflies transcripts into the SecondBrain vault; audit vault filenames for drift against Fireflies titles. Idempotent. Use when the user says \"sync meetings\", \"pull latest meetings\", \"/meeting-sync\", \"update meeting notes\", \"any new meetings?\", or when invoked by weekly-review. Also for filename drift — \"are any meeting files misnamed?\", \"did a meeting get renamed after export?\", \"why is this meeting titled differently in Fireflies than in the vault?\", \"audit the meeting filenames\". Do not use to rename generic titles inside Fireflies (use name-meetings) or to search what was said in transcripts (use pp-fireflies).\n- memory-write: Writes, updates, removes, and recalls facts in the two-tier memory system (~/.claude/global-memory/ and ~/.claude/projects/<slug>/memory/). Use for write requests such as \"remember this\", \"note that\", \"update memory\", \"save this\", and \"forget about\", and for recall requests such as \"did we already…?\", \"what did we decide about X?\", and \"do you remember…?\". Picks the tier, dedups, and enforces caps; recall checks the injected snapshot, then memsearch. Do not use for a story or lesson worth a vault note (use capture-insight) or an article to keep (use ingest-resource).\n- name-meetings: Detects Fireflies meetings with generic titles (empty, \"Zoom Meeting\", \"Recording\", date-only, etc.), proposes structured names based on AI summary + participants, and applies approved renames via the Fireflies API. Use when user says \"/name-meetings\", \"name my meetings\", \"rename untitled meetings\", \"fix meeting titles\", \"my meetings have no names\", or when invoked by meeting-sync. Do not use to sync transcripts or audit vault filenames (use meeting-sync).\n- new-project: Scaffold a new project in DEV the standard way: git repo, AGENTS.md with the session routine, CLAUDE.md pointer, docs/STATUS.md, .gitignore, private GitHub repo under erash11. Use when Eric starts a new project or says 'set this up as a repo'. Do not use to log an idea in the portfolio registry (use project) or to sync repos that already exist (use dev-sync).\n- next-decision: Drill open decisions one at a time — present the most important decision not yet clarified, give the top four choices, state a preference, ask the user. Use when the user says \"next decision\", \"one decision at a time\", or a plan has several unresolved choices. Differentiator: forward-looking; the decisions skill is retrospective (choices already made). Can be invoked with /next-decision.\n- pbir-report-builder: Generates Power BI report pages, visuals, and IBCS-compliant variance charts by writing PBIR JSON into PBIP project folders. Use when the user wants to create a Power BI page, add visuals, generate KPI cards, build charts, tables or dashboard layouts, or mentions 'PBIR', 'IBCS', 'variance chart', 'variance table', 'actual vs plan'. Do not use for PowerPoint decks (use five-slides-generator) or HTML dashboards outside Power BI.\n- persistent-localhost: Start, stop, restart, and inspect durable localhost dev servers on macOS as user LaunchAgents, so they outlive the agent's shell and auto-restart on crash. Use whenever an agent must run a dev server, API, or any long-lived local process on a port: \"start the dev server\", \"run it on localhost\", \"keep the server running\", \"the server keeps dying\", \"restart the app on port 5111\". Never start servers with nohup, &, disown, setsid, or run_in_background; those die with the shell or become unfindable orphans.\n- pp-catapult-openfield-connect\n- pp-fireflies\n- pp-zotero-research-library: Your entire Zotero research library, searchable offline — version-based incremental sync into SQLite with full-text search no other Zotero tool has. Use when the user says `search my Zotero library`, `what does my library say about`, `find papers about`, `sync my Zotero library`, `what did I add to Zotero this week`, `get the citation for`, `use zotero-research-library`, `run zotero cli`. Do not use for a substantive sport-science question that needs a grounded, cited answer (use sports-science-research-grounding).\n- project: Manage the portfolio project registry (ideas, updates, ranking, promotion, dev stages) and render/publish the dashboard. Use when the user says \"/project\", \"new idea:\", \"log progress on\", \"project update\", \"add to my project inbox\", \"show my projects\", \"re-rank my projects\", \"set the stage on\", \"mark <project> as spec'd/planned/shipped\". Do not use for the DEV drift dashboard (use project-triage) or to scaffold a new repo (use new-project).\n- project-triage: Generate the DEV/ project triage HTML dashboard for today or a backfill date, surfacing drift between curated and current state. Use when the user says \"project triage\", \"/project-triage\", \"run my project review\", \"dev dashboard\", \"what's open in DEV\", \"morning review\", or asks to see the open-projects HTML. Do not use to log ideas or progress in the portfolio registry (use project) or for one project's meeting brief (use meeting-prep).\n- scroll-craft: Build a premium, scroll-driven interactive landing page for any business: a service company, a physical product, a food brand, a drink brand. Scroll becomes the timeline. Video scrubs frame by frame under the wheel, sections pin and advance, rails pan sideways, headlines assemble line by line, the page ground shifts colour as you travel, and the pointer moves things that are not scrolling. Interviews the human first (their vibe, their journey, one unbroken world or distinct scenes, and what assets they already own), then picks a page grammar and a signature move so no two builds share a skeleton, generates photoreal assets through kie.ai or builds from the user's own footage and photos, writes real semantic HTML on a design-system floor, and verifies the result by screenshotting its own scroll. Use for \"scrollytelling\", \"scroll animation site\", \"a site where scrolling plays a video\", \"Apple-style landing page\", \"3D scroll world\", \"interactive landing page\", \"make my brand a scroll experience\", \"make it feel different\", \"this looks like a template\", \"a unique scroll site\", or any request for a site that should feel like an experience rather than a document.\n- sports-science-research-grounding: Use when the user asks a substantive question about sport science, return to play or sport, rehabilitation, strength and conditioning, workload, recovery, testing, athlete health, injury, performance, or wants a research-backed answer, and for Zotero full-library batch processing. Searches the user's Zotero library first, clearly labels external peer-reviewed fallback sources, and applies tier-aware model routing during bounded batch work. Do not use for sports scores or schedules (use pp-espn) or for a simple Zotero search or sync (use pp-zotero-research-library).\n- startup: Start of day, or sitting down at either machine: sync this machine against GitHub, then report where the last session left off and wait. Use when Eric says '/startup', 'good morning', 'starting up', 'where did I leave off', 'what was I working on', 'picking this back up', or opens a session without naming a task. Do not use for the end-of-session sign-off (use closeout).\n- status-report: Build the every-two-weeks Applied Performance status report for Kenny Boyd and Chris Ruf — paste-ready email, one-page PDF, internal record. Use when the user says \"/status-report\", \"build my status report\", \"time for the Chris report\", \"biweekly update\", \"status update for Chris and Kenny\", \"what do I send Chris this week\", or when a scheduled report date arrives. Do not use for a single meeting brief (use meeting-prep) or the end-of-day supervisor log interview (use interview-me).\n- weekly-review\n- codex:rescue: Delegate investigation, an explicit fix request, or follow-up rescue work to the Codex rescue subagent\n- codex:setup: Check whether the local Codex CLI is ready and optionally toggle the stop-time review gate\n- superpowers:brainstorming: You MUST use this before any creative work - creating features, building components, adding functionality, or modifying behavior. Explores user intent, requirements and design before implementation.\n- superpowers:dispatching-parallel-agents: Use when facing 2+ independent tasks that can be worked on without shared state or sequential dependencies\n- superpowers:executing-plans: Use when you have a written implementation plan to execute in a separate session with review checkpoints\n- superpowers:finishing-a-development-branch: Use when implementation is complete, all tests pass, and you need to decide how to integrate the work\n- superpowers:receiving-code-review\n- superpowers:requesting-code-review\n- superpowers:subagent-driven-development: Use when executing implementation plans with independent tasks in the current session\n- superpowers:systematic-debugging\n- superpowers:test-driven-development: Use when implementing any feature or bugfix, before writing implementation code\n- superpowers:using-git-worktrees: Use when starting feature work that needs isolation from current workspace or before executing implementation plans - ensures an isolated workspace exists via native tools or git worktree fallback\n- superpowers:using-superpowers\n- superpowers:verification-before-completion: Use when about to claim work is complete, fixed, or passing, before committing or creating PRs - requires running verification commands and confirming output before making any success claims; evidence before assertions always\n- superpowers:writing-plans: Use when you have a spec or requirements for a multi-step task, before touching code\n- superpowers:writing-skills\n- last30days:last30days\n- context-mode:context-mode\n- context-mode:ctx-doctor\n- context-mode:ctx-index\n- context-mode:ctx-insight\n- context-mode:ctx-purge\n- context-mode:ctx-search\n- context-mode:ctx-stats\n- context-mode:ctx-upgrade\n- codex:codex-cli-runtime: Internal helper contract for calling the codex-companion runtime from Claude Code\n- codex:codex-result-handling\n- codex:gpt-5-4-prompting\n- mattpocock-skills:diagnosing-bugs\n- mattpocock-skills:tdd\n- mattpocock-skills:prototype: Build a throwaway prototype to answer a design question. Use when the user wants to sanity-check whether a state model or logic feels right, or explore what a UI should look like.\n- mattpocock-skills:research\n- mattpocock-skills:domain-modeling\n- mattpocock-skills:codebase-design\n- mattpocock-skills:code-review\n- mattpocock-skills:resolving-merge-conflicts\n- mattpocock-skills:wizard\n- mattpocock-skills:grilling: Grill the user relentlessly about a plan, decision, or idea. Use when the user wants to stress-test their thinking, or uses any 'grill' trigger phrases.\n- mattpocock-skills:writing-for-agents\n- watch:watch: Watch a video (URL or local path). Downloads with yt-dlp, extracts auto-scaled frames with ffmpeg, pulls the transcript from captions (or Whisper API fallback), and hands the result to Claude so it can answer questions about what's in the video.\n- dataviz: Use this skill whenever you are about to create ANY chart, graph, plot, dashboard, or data visualization, in ANY output medium — an HTML or React artifact, inline SVG, plotting code in any library (matplotlib, plotly, d3, Recharts, …), an image/PNG you will render and upload, or a chart shared into Slack. Read it BEFORE writing the first line of chart code, choosing chart colors, building a stat tile / meter / KPI row, or laying out a dashboard. Produces visualizations that read as one system — elegant, accessible, consistent in light and dark — using a brand-neutral placeholder palette you swap for your own. Teaches a design-system-agnostic method: a form heuristic, a color formula with a runnable validator, mark specs, and interaction rules. A validated default palette is documented in `references/palette.md` — swap that file's values for your brand's. Triggers on: \"chart\", \"graph\", \"plot\", \"data viz\", \"visualization\", \"dashboard\", \"analytics\", \"visualize data\", \"categorical colors\", \"sequential / diverging palette\", \"stat tile\", \"sparkline\", \"heatmap\", \"legend\", \"axis\", \"tooltip\", \"chart colors\", \"color by series\".\n- update-config: Use this skill to configure the Claude Code harness via settings.json. Automated behaviors (\"from now on when X\", \"each time X\", \"whenever X\", \"before/after X\") require hooks configured in settings.json - the harness executes these, not Claude, so memory/preferences cannot fulfill them. Also use for: permissions (\"allow X\", \"add permission\", \"move permission to\"), env vars (\"set X=Y\"), hook troubleshooting, or any changes to settings.json/settings.local.json files. Examples: \"allow npm commands\", \"add bq permission to global settings\", \"move permission to user settings\", \"set DEBUG=true\", \"when claude stops show X\". For simple settings like theme/model, suggest the /config command.\n- keybindings-help: Use when the user wants to customize keyboard shortcuts, rebind keys, add chord bindings, or modify ~/.claude/keybindings.json. Examples: \"rebind ctrl+s\", \"add a chord shortcut\", \"change the submit key\", \"customize keybindings\".\n- simplify: Review the changed code for reuse, simplification, efficiency, and altitude cleanups, then apply the fixes. Quality only — it does not hunt for bugs; use /code-review for that.\n- fewer-permission-prompts: Scan your transcripts for common read-only Bash and MCP tool calls, then add a prioritized allowlist to project .claude/settings.json to reduce permission prompts.\n- loop: Run a prompt or slash command on a recurring interval (e.g. /loop 5m /foo). Omit the interval to let the model self-pace. - When the user wants to set up a recurring task, poll for status, or run something repeatedly on an interval (e.g. \"check the deploy every 5 minutes\", \"keep running /babysit-prs\"). Do NOT invoke for one-off tasks.\n- schedule: Create, update, list, or run scheduled cloud agents (routines) that execute on a cron schedule. - When the user wants to schedule a recurring cloud agent, set up automated tasks, create a cron job for Claude Code, or manage their scheduled agents/routines. Also use when the user wants a one-time scheduled run (\"run this once at 3pm\", \"remind me to check X tomorrow\").\n- claude-api: Reference for the Claude API / Anthropic SDK — model ids, pricing, params, streaming, tool use, MCP, agents, caching, token counting, model migration.\nTRIGGER — read BEFORE opening the target file; don't skip because it \"looks like a one-liner\" — whenever: the prompt names Claude/Anthropic in any form (Claude, Anthropic, Fable, Opus, Sonnet, Haiku, `anthropic`, `@anthropic-ai`, `claude-*`, `us.anthropic.*`, `[1m]`); the user asks about an LLM (pricing/model choice/limits/caching) — never answer from memory; OR the task is LLM-shaped with provider unstated (agent/MCP/tool-definition/multi-agent/RAG/LLM-judge/computer-use; generate/summarize/extract/classify/rewrite/converse over NL; debugging refusals/cutoffs/streaming/tool-calls/tokens).\nSKIP only when another provider is being worked on (overrides all triggers): OpenAI/GPT/Gemini/Llama/Mistral/Cohere/Ollama named in the query; OR `grep -rE 'openai|langchain_openai|google.generativeai|genai|mistralai|cohere|ollama'` over the project hits (run this grep FIRST if no provider named — don't Read the file).\n- run: Launch and drive this project's app to see a change working. Use when asked to run, start, or screenshot the app, or to confirm a change works in the real app (not just tests). First looks for a project skill that already covers launching the app; otherwise falls back to built-in patterns per project type (CLI, server, TUI, Electron, browser-driven, library).\n- init: Initialize a new CLAUDE.md file with codebase documentation\n- security-review","skillCount":97,"isInitial":true,"names":["anti-sleep","backup","brain-audit","claude-stack-doctor","claudex-loop","claudex-route","cli-hub-meta-skill","closeout","code-review","codex-build","codex-review","CreateSkill","design-taste-frontend","dev-sync","doctor-plus","fireflies-intake","five-slides-generator","forecast-capture","game-film-timestamps","graphify","harvest","html-to-pdf","impeccable","improve-system","ingest-book","ingest-resource","interview-me","library-advisor","meeting-prep","meeting-sync","memory-write","name-meetings","new-project","next-decision","pbir-report-builder","persistent-localhost","pp-catapult-openfield-connect","pp-fireflies","pp-zotero-research-library","project","project-triage","scroll-craft","sports-science-research-grounding","startup","status-report","weekly-review","codex:rescue","codex:setup","superpowers:brainstorming","superpowers:dispatching-parallel-agents","superpowers:executing-plans","superpowers:finishing-a-development-branch","superpowers:receiving-code-review","superpowers:requesting-code-review","superpowers:subagent-driven-development","superpowers:systematic-debugging","superpowers:test-driven-development","superpowers:using-git-worktrees","superpowers:using-superpowers","superpowers:verification-before-completion","superpowers:writing-plans","superpowers:writing-skills","last30days:last30days","context-mode:context-mode","context-mode:ctx-doctor","c